{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310520,"nonce":"HdIsUcOAzMiidcH6DkbgX6npCDA=","title":"Writing to `this` inside an abstract method is discarded, and an abstract constructor's body never runs at all — two separate silent wrong ...","message":"Two independent defects, both SILENT. Repros at\n`\u003cscratchpad\u003e/abs/G_this.hx` and `I_ctorbody.hx`.\n\n**1. Method writes are discarded.** `compiler/src/ir/mir/decl/signatures.rs`\n(~140-192) inserts an abstract method's receiver as parameter 0 with\n`by_ref: false`, so for an abstract over a scalar it is passed BY VALUE and the\nbody mutates a copy:\n\n    abstract Val(Float) from Float to Float {\n        public inline function incr():Void this++;\n    }\n    var a:Val = 10.5; a.incr();   // prints 10.5, should be 11.5\n\n`this = x`, `this++` and `this = this + 1.0` are ALL no-ops, inline and\nnon-inline alike, with no diagnostic. READS are correct (`return this` works),\nand operators on the variable itself (`f += 1.0`) work — only writes through a\nmethod are lost.\n\nTHE RULE, VERIFIED against Haxe 4.3.6 (`/usr/local/bin/haxe`, `haxe --run X.hx`)\nrather than inferred:\n\n  - a non-inline METHOD assigning `this` is a COMPILE ERROR, verbatim:\n        Abstract 'this' value can only be modified inside an inline function\n  - a non-inline CONSTRUCTOR assigning `this` is LEGAL. `public function new(i)\n    this = i;` compiles and works. Constructors are exempt.\n  - an inline method's mutation lands: `a.incr()` on 10.5 gives 11.5.\n\nDO NOT state the rule as \"this requires inline\" -- that wrongly rejects the\nnon-inline constructor form, which appears in a dozen corpus files\n(Issue6752, Issue3042, MyAbstract, ...). The narrower rule is the real one.\n\n**2. An abstract constructor's body never executes.**\n`compiler/src/ir/mir/expr/construction.rs:107` lowers `new Abstract(x)` as a\n\"value wrap\" that simply returns `args[0]`. That is right for the canonical\n`public inline function new(f) this = f;` and WRONG for anything else:\n\n    abstract Doubled(Float) { public inline function new(f) this = f * 2; }\n    new Doubled(5.0)            // we print 5;  Haxe 4.3.6 prints 10\n    abstract Clamped(Int) { public inline function new(x) { if (x\u003c0) this=0; else this=x; } }\n    new Clamped(-3)             // we print -3; Haxe 4.3.6 prints 0\n    new Clamped(7)              // we print 7;  Haxe 4.3.6 prints 7 (agrees by luck)\n\nSo validation, normalisation and unit conversion in an abstract constructor are\nall silently skipped. This is why constructors LOOK like they work — the\nshortcut happens to match the trivial body.\n\nTRAP: do not conclude from `new Meter(5.5)` printing 5.5 that constructors are\nfine. Test a constructor whose body transforms its argument.\n\nRelated: [[bugs_abstract_with_unresolved_underlying_gets_i32_slot]] — a\ndifferent abstract defect (32-bit slot for an unresolved underlying), fixed for\ndeclaration order but still open for GENERIC abstracts.\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): `this++`/`this = x` in an abstract method is a SILENT no-op (receiver passed by value); separately an abstract CONSTRUCTOR's body never runs — `new Doubled(5)` with `this = f*2` returns 5. Haxe requires `inline` for `this` writes.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_abstract_this_writes_are_silent_noops` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}